DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Condition attribute objects are used to specify parameters to
     pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonndd__iinniitt().

     The pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonnddaattttrr__iinniitt() function initializes a condition attribute
     object with the default attributes.

     The pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonnddaattttrr__ddeessttrrooyy() function destroys a condition attribute
     object.

     The pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonnddaattttrr__ggeettcclloocckk() function will put the value of the clock
     attribute from _a_t_t_r into the memory area pointed to by _c_l_o_c_k___i_d.  The
     pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonnddaattttrr__sseettcclloocckk() function will set the clock attribute of _a_t_t_r
     to the value specified in _c_l_o_c_k___i_d.  The clock attribute affects the in‐
     terpretation of _a_b_s_t_i_m_e in pthread_cond_timedwait(3) and may be set to
     CLOCK_REALTIME (default) or CLOCK_MONOTONIC.

     The pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonnddaattttrr__ggeettppsshhaarreedd() function will put the value of the
     process-shared attribute from _a_t_t_r into the memory area pointed to by
     _p_s_h_a_r_e_d.  The pptthhrreeaadd__ccoonnddaattttrr__sseettppsshhaarreedd() function will set the
     process-shared attribute of _a_t_t_r to the value specified in _p_s_h_a_r_e_d.  The
     argument _p_s_h_a_r_e_d may have one of the following values:

     PTHREAD_PROCESS_PRIVATE  The condition variable it is attached to may
                              only be accessed by threads in the same process
                              as the one that created the object.

     PTHREAD_PROCESS_SHARED   The condition variable it is attached to may be
                              accessed by threads in processes other than the
                              one that created the object.
     See libthr(3) for details of the implementation of shared condition vari‐
     ables, and their limitations.

